# Committed on purpose, same convention as `.env.test` (see that file's
# header): Vite loads `.env.[mode]` automatically, and `playwright.config.ts`
# runs its `webServer` as `vite dev --mode e2e`. Nothing here is a secret.
#
# `VITE_SVC_CONFIG_URL`/`VITE_SVC_I18N_URL` deliberately point at ports with
# NOTHING listening: `web/e2e/**` tests never talk to a real backend at all —
# every request either gets intercepted by Playwright's `page.route()`
# (`web/e2e/mockApis.ts`, real bundle/rule content, but never a live
# process) or, for the one test that wants a genuinely unreachable svc-i18n
# (Part 2 item 6, "test the i18n service being unreachable"), is left
# un-mocked and simply fails to connect — which is exactly the scenario
# under test. Using fake ports (rather than real ones with nothing bound)
# means a stray local `docker compose up` can never accidentally make a
# "backend unreachable" test pass for the wrong reason.
VITE_OIDC_ISSUER=http://localhost:8080/auth/realms/gadonghr
VITE_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=web
VITE_OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5183/auth/callback
VITE_OIDC_AUDIENCE=gadonghr-services
VITE_SVC_CONFIG_URL=http://localhost:4910
VITE_SVC_I18N_URL=http://localhost:4911
VITE_SVC_AUDIT_URL=http://localhost:4912
VITE_SVC_DOCS_URL=http://localhost:4913
VITE_SVC_AUTHZ_URL=http://localhost:4914
VITE_SVC_NOTIFY_URL=http://localhost:4915

# THE reason this whole suite can authenticate at all (see `web/README.md`
# and `src/auth/devBypass.ts`): production Keycloak has exactly one real
# user whose first login forces a password change + OTP enrolment, so it
# cannot be scripted, and must not be. `vite dev --mode e2e` keeps Vite's
# `import.meta.env.DEV` true, which is what actually gates the dev-bypass
# code into existence at all (`src/env.ts`'s `DEV_BYPASS_ENABLED`) — a
# `vite build` bundle can never do this, by construction, regardless of
# this flag (`devBypass.build.test.tsx` proves it).
VITE_DEV_BYPASS=true
